1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,180
“The cart disappeared.”

2
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:07,560
BugReplay turns browser symptoms into evidence
you can actually run.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,380
Give it a URL and the expected behavior.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:16,840
The model explores the interface, learns from
browser observations, and records the complete
setup.

5
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:20,220
No source code. No hidden trigger recipe.

6
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,280
This investigation found the disappearing cart.

7
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:25,780
Then the model steps aside.

8
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:29,300
The saved Playwright test runs twice in clean
browsers.

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,360
Both fail on the intended cart preservation
assertion.

10
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:36,720
Two items become zero.

11
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:38,140
That is the proof.

12
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:41,140
Change only the base URL.

13
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:45,940
The exact same test passes on the corrected
shop.

14
00:00:47,040 --> 00:00:49,120
Same steps. Same assertion.

15
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:55,400
You get the test, report, screenshots, and
original recordings.

16
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,520
Neatlogs exposed malformed responses that
interrupted exploration.

17
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:04,940
We added bounded recovery, then traced the real
rerun.

18
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:08,300
Entire links the fix to its checkpoint and diff.

19
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:13,140
CFO AI models pricing and separates measured
usage from estimates.

20
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,720
Failed attempts count too.

21
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,300
One controlled shop. One defect class. One of
three final buggy investigations confirmed it.

22
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:31,720
Complete cash billing remains unknown.
BugReplay. From a vague report to a verified
reproduction.
